Paging (Operating System)

Definition:
Paging is a memory management technique in which a process is divided into fixed-size blocks called pages and main memory is divided into blocks of the same size called frames. Pages are loaded into available frames in physical memory.

Basic Concept:

Logical memory is divided into pages

Physical memory is divided into frames

Each page can be placed in any free frame

Address translation is done using a page table

Address Translation in Paging:
The logical address is divided into two parts:

Page number

Offset

The page number is used to index the page table, which provides the frame number. The frame number combined with the offset forms the physical address.

Page Table:
A page table is a data structure maintained by the operating system that maps pages to frames and supports address translation.

Advantages of Paging:

Eliminates external fragmentation

Supports virtual memory

Efficient memory utilization

Simplifies memory allocation

Allows non-contiguous memory allocation

Disadvantages of Paging:

Internal fragmentation may occur

Page table consumes memory

Address translation overhead

Increased memory access time

Internal Fragmentation in Paging:
Occurs when the last page of a process does not fully utilize the allocated frame, leaving unused space.

